        </nav>

        <h2>1. Controller and data protection officer</h2>

        <h3>1.1 Controller</h3>
        <p>
          The controller for the processing of personal data within the meaning of Art. 4(7) GDPR
          is Stellar Foundry GmbH, Thomas-Mann-Stra&szlig;e 19, 53111 Bonn, Germany, represented by
          its managing director Ms Marion Hillebrecht.
        </p>
        <p>Email address: <a href="mailto:privacy@pelton.app">privacy@pelton.app</a></p>

        <h3>1.2 Data protection officer</h3>
        <p>
          We are not required to appoint a data protection officer under Art. 37 GDPR and
          &sect; 38 BDSG. The conditions that would trigger that obligation are not met. Our core
          activity does not consist of regular and systematic monitoring of data subjects on a
          large scale, nor of large-scale processing of special categories of personal data
          (Art. 37(1)(b) and (c) GDPR); the processing carried out in connection with the website
          is limited in nature and does not reach the statutory thresholds, in particular the
          threshold in &sect; 38(1) BDSG of at least 20 people permanently engaged in the automated
          processing of personal data.
        </p>
        <p>
          We nonetheless carry out the duties of a data protection officer ourselves and are
          available for any data protection question. You can reach us at any time at the email
          address given in section 1.1. Your enquiries are treated confidentially. We review
          regularly whether the obligation to appoint a data protection officer has changed, in
          particular if the number of people permanently engaged in automated data processing rises
          or if our processing activities expand.
        </p>

        <h2>2. Separation between the website and the application</h2>
        <p>
          Our website and the Pelton application we provide are technically separate from each
          other. That separation is the starting point for every section that follows, which is why
          we set it out first.
        </p>

        <h3>2.1 The website</h3>
        <p>
          This policy applies to the website at pelton.app and to its subdomains (in particular
          docs.pelton.app and themes.pelton.app). Visiting these pages generates technical access
          data that your browser transmits automatically. That processing is the subject of
          sections 3 to 8 of this policy.
        </p>
        <p>
          The website is also the showroom for the application: it describes Pelton, explains how
          to set it up, hosts the documentation and provides the theme gallery. The privacy policy,
          the terms of use and the information about our services are all available there.
        </p>

        <h3>2.2 The Pelton application</h3>
        <p>
          Once installed, the Pelton application runs entirely locally on your device. It transmits
          no data to us and makes no contact with our servers. There is no channel back to us: no
          server connection, no tracking module, no device fingerprinting for marketing purposes
          and no transmission of usage logs.
        </p>
        <p>
          Your email accounts, credentials and messages are processed exclusively between your
          device and your own email provider. We are neither involved in that processing nor able
          to access that data. Your email provider is solely responsible for its own processing.
        </p>
        <p>
          The AI features Pelton can use, where you choose to use them, are likewise your
          responsibility. Pelton is a bridge between you and the AI provider you select. We operate
          no model and are not a controller for that processing. If you use these features, Pelton
          sends the contents of your requests to the AI provider you configured. Because you choose
          the provider, the account and the endpoint yourself, those providers' privacy policies
          apply. We have no influence over the content or the processing operations and are not a
          processor in this respect either.
        </p>
        <p>
          Since we process no personal data in connection with the application, we cannot accept
          responsibility for that part. Please direct requests for access, erasure or rectification
          concerning data processed in the application to the provider responsible in each case.
        </p>
        <p>
          We do not record whether, how often or with which accounts you use Pelton. We keep no
          licence, activation or telemetry database, and therefore cannot tell you what data Pelton
          processes on your machine.
        </p>

        <h3>2.3 The website as a distribution channel for software and themes</h3>
        <p>
          The website plays three roles in making the software available. First, it explains how
          the application works and how to set it up. Second, it links to release files hosted on
          an external platform (section 8). Third, at themes.pelton.app it provides a gallery of
          themes and theme packs to download. In all three roles we process only the technical data
          described in sections 3 and 4.
        </p>

        <h2>3. Principles governing the processing</h2>
        <p>
          We process as little personal data as possible. The website has no user accounts, no
          sign-in, no comment function, no newsletter sign-up and no contact forms. No special
          categories of personal data within the meaning of Art. 9 GDPR are collected. No automated
          decision-making, including profiling, within the meaning of Art. 22 GDPR takes place.
        </p>

        <h2>4. What data arises when you visit the website</h2>
        <p>
          When you open the website, your browser transmits technical data to the server delivering
          it. This data is technically necessary to deliver the page; it arises on every request to
          any website, whether or not we evaluate it.
        </p>
        <p>In detail, we process the following categories:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>the IP address of the requesting device;</li>
          <li>the date and time of the request and the time zone;</li>
          <li>the URL requested, the file requested and the volume of data transferred;</li>
          <li>the HTTP method and the HTTP status code of the response;</li>
          <li>the referrer URL, that is the page visited before, where your browser sends it;</li>
          <li>browser type, browser version and the language setting;</li>
          <li>operating system and device category (desktop, mobile, tablet);</li>
          <li>the screen resolution, where it arises as part of delivery.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          This data is not combined with other sources, and no user profiles linked to your
          identity are created. We do not attribute it to any particular person, nor could we
          without further information.
        </p>

        <h3>4.1 Retention</h3>
        <p>
          We do not store this access data permanently ourselves. Where it is needed for error
          analysis or to fend off attacks, it is held briefly by our host and then deleted; the
          specific periods follow from our host's own information in section 5. We have no direct
          influence over how long the host retains it.
        </p>

        <h2>5. Hosting of the main website (Cloudflare)</h2>
        <p>
          The main website at pelton.app is hosted and delivered through Cloudflare Pages. The
          provider is Cloudflare, Inc., 101 Townsend St., San Francisco, CA 94107, USA. Cloudflare
          also operates a worldwide network for delivering content and fending off attacks.
        </p>
        <p>
          When you open the website, Cloudflare processes the technical data described in
          section 4: IP address, date and time of the request, the URL or files requested, referrer
          URL, browser type and version, operating system, volume of data transferred and HTTP
          status code.
        </p>
        <p>
          The legal basis is Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR. Our legitimate interest lies in delivering the
          website correctly, quickly and securely, in keeping it stable under varying load, and in
          fending off attacks (such as denial-of-service attacks, scraping and unauthorised
          access). The processing is technically necessary for delivery; without processing the IP
          address, a request from the internet cannot be answered. You may object to this
          processing (section 12), though in practice that means the website cannot be used.
        </p>
        <p>
          Cloudflare acts for us as a processor under Art. 28 GDPR. A data processing agreement is
          in place with Cloudflare. Cloudflare processes the data solely on our documented
          instructions and in accordance with the GDPR.
        </p>
        <p>
          Any transfer to the USA takes place on the basis of the EU-U.S. Data Privacy Framework
          and/or the EU standard contractual clauses under Art. 46(2)(c) GDPR. Details are in
          section 11.
        </p>
        <p>
          Cloudflare stores the log data arising during delivery for a limited period and then
          deletes it. The exact periods and the security measures applied follow from Cloudflare's
          own information.
        </p>
        <p>
          Further information:
          <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">cloudflare.com/privacypolicy</a>
        </p>

        <h2>6. Documentation, theme gallery and audience measurement</h2>

        <h3>6.1 Hosting of the documentation and the theme gallery (GitHub Pages)</h3>
        <p>
          Our documentation at docs.pelton.app and our theme gallery at themes.pelton.app are
          static, pre-generated websites hosted through GitHub Pages. The provider is GitHub, Inc.,
          88 Colin P. Kelly Jr. Street, San Francisco, CA 94107, USA, a Microsoft Corporation
          company.
        </p>
        <p>
          These pages have no application backend that receives your data. All content, fonts, CSS
          files, JavaScript files, images and previews and, in the case of the theme gallery, the
          downloadable theme files (.peltontheme) are self-hosted and delivered from the same
          origin. No third-party CDNs, no third-party analytics or tracking services and no cookies
          are used. Any search or filtering in the gallery runs entirely in your browser and
          transmits nothing to us. Downloading a theme likewise comes from the same origin and
          involves no further third party.
        </p>
        <p>
          When you open these pages, GitHub processes the technical data your browser transmits
          automatically, comparable to section 4 (in particular IP address, date and time of the
          request, the URL requested, referrer URL and browser metadata), and may log visitors' IP
          addresses for security purposes, whether or not you are signed in to GitHub. We have no
          access to this data and receive no analysis derived from it.
        </p>
        <p>
          The legal basis is Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR. Our legitimate interest lies in making the
          technical documentation and the theme gallery available reliably, economically and
          worldwide. Here too the processing is technically necessary for delivery.
        </p>
        <p>
          GitHub is an independent controller for this processing. Any transfer to the USA takes
          place on the basis of the EU-U.S. Data Privacy Framework and/or the EU standard
          contractual clauses. Details are available at
          <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">docs.github.com</a>
        </p>

        <h3>6.2 Audience measurement with Cloudflare Analytics</h3>
        <p>
          We use the server-side analytics built into our hosting at Cloudflare in order to
          understand use of this website in aggregate, for example page views, referrers, country
          of origin and device category. The purpose is to understand which content is read, which
          documentation pages are missing and how the website can be improved.
        </p>
        <p>
          These statistics are generated server-side from the technical access data described in
          section 4. No additional analytics script is loaded into your browser, no cookies are
          set, and no information is stored on or read from your device. No tracking across
          sessions or across different websites takes place. Recognising you across several visits
          is not possible.
        </p>
        <p>
          Cloudflare Analytics works from the connection data that arises anyway when a page is
          requested. Metrics are derived from that data without any profile of an individual
          visitor being created. The analysis is therefore not based on access to your device, but
          on connection data already held by the host.
        </p>
        <p>
          The legal basis is Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR. Our legitimate interest lies in measuring and
          improving the reach, content and usability of our website and in establishing which parts
          of the documentation are actually used. An interest in improving one's own offering is
          recognised in law and outweighs visitors' interests here, because no personal analysis
          and no profiling take place.
        </p>
        <p>
          The server-side analysis does not access your browser. No cookies are set, no data is
          stored in or read from your device's local storage or session storage, and no script is
          loaded that runs on your device. The conditions of &sect; 25(1) TDDDG, namely storing
          information on the device or accessing information already stored on it, are therefore
          not met. No consent needs to be obtained for audience measurement, and no consent banner
          is shown. This distinction is explained in section 10.3.
        </p>
        <p>
          The aggregated statistics contain no personal data within the meaning of Art. 4(1) GDPR;
          individual visitors cannot be identified from them. Your IP address is processed for this
          purpose only temporarily by Cloudflare as a processor, to the extent technically
          necessary to derive the metrics. Details are in Cloudflare's privacy policy.
        </p>

        <h3>6.3 No other analytics or marketing services</h3>
        <p>
          Beyond the server-generated statistics described above, we use no analytics, tracking or
          marketing services. In particular, your behaviour is not analysed for advertising
          purposes, no advertising networks are embedded, no advertising or retargeting cookies are
          set, and no data is sold to data brokers. We also embed no social media plugins that
          would transmit data to third parties when a page loads.
        </p>

        <h2>7. Fonts, images and other assets</h2>
        <p>
          All fonts and static assets, that is stylesheets, scripts, images, icons and preview
          images, are self-hosted and delivered from the same origin as the page itself. No
          connection is made to Google Fonts or to any other external font or asset provider. This
          prevents from the outset any connection being opened to a further third party, and your
          IP address being transmitted to them, when a page loads. The only exception is the avatar
          images described in section 8.1, which are loaded as part of the GitHub data request.
        </p>

        <h2>8. External channels: the GitHub API and downloads</h2>

        <h3>8.1 Retrieving project information through the GitHub API</h3>
        <p>
          To show current release information, repository statistics and contributors, this website
          retrieves data directly through the GitHub API and may load avatar images from GitHub's
          servers. The provider is GitHub, Inc., 88 Colin P. Kelly Jr. Street, San Francisco,
          CA 94107, USA, a Microsoft Corporation company.
        </p>
        <p>
          In doing so, your IP address is transmitted to GitHub when the relevant pages load,
          together with browser and request data. This happens automatically when the page opens,
          not only when you click. We receive no copy of that data in the process; we receive only
          the publicly available project information that GitHub returns to any caller.
        </p>
        <p>
          The legal basis is Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR. Our legitimate interest lies in displaying current
          and accurate information about our open-source software. GitHub processes the data
          independently; it is an independent controller in this respect, not our processor. Any
          transfer to the USA takes place on the basis of the EU-U.S. Data Privacy Framework and/or
          the EU standard contractual clauses. GitHub's privacy policy:
          <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">docs.github.com</a>
        </p>

        <h3>8.2 Providing downloads</h3>
        <p>
          The download button points to release files hosted at GitHub. When a download starts,
          your IP address and request data are transmitted to GitHub. What is said in section 8.1
          applies here too; GitHub carries out that processing independently and on its own
          responsibility.
        </p>
        <p>
          The download button detects your operating system in order to suggest the right file to
          you. That detection happens entirely in your browser, from the identifier your browser
          transmits. The result is neither transmitted to us nor stored by us. GitHub alone is
          responsible for delivering the file itself; nothing is transmitted to us in the process.
          Please note that GitHub's own terms of use and privacy provisions apply additionally to
          downloads through GitHub.
        </p>

        <h2>9. Contacting us by email</h2>
        <p>
          If you contact us by email, we process the data you provide, that is your email address,
          the content of your message and any further details you give such as your name or
          organisation, in order to deal with your enquiry. That includes reading your enquiry,
          answering it, asking follow-up questions where needed and documenting the matter through
          to its conclusion.
        </p>
        <p>
          The legal basis is Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR (legitimate interest in answering enquiries) and,
          where your message concerns entering into or performing a contract, Art. 6(1)(b) GDPR
          (performance of a contract or pre-contractual measures).
        </p>
        <p>
          We keep correspondence for as long as it is needed to deal with the matter. Beyond that
          we store it only where statutory retention obligations apply, in particular the
          commercial and tax retention periods under &sect; 257 HGB and &sect; 147 AO. Once the
          relevant periods expire we delete the data, unless you have expressly agreed to longer
          storage.
        </p>
        <p>
          Our email service is operated by Namecheap, Inc., 4600 East Washington Street, Suite 305,
          Phoenix, AZ 85034, USA. Namecheap acts for us as a processor under Art. 28 GDPR in this
          respect and processes the correspondence data solely on our instructions. The transfer to
          the USA takes place on the basis of the EU standard contractual clauses, which form part
          of Namecheap's data processing addendum. Details are in section 11.
        </p>
        <p>
          Please note that you do not have to disclose special categories of personal data (such as
          health data) to us in order to make an enquiry. Please do not send us confidential
          content that is not needed to deal with your matter; we will delete such content
          immediately on request.
        </p>

        <h2>10. Cookies, local storage and consent</h2>

        <h3>10.1 No cookies, no local storage</h3>
        <p>
          This website sets no cookies and stores no data in your browser's local storage or
          session storage. A consent banner is therefore unnecessary and is not shown. The search
          and filter functions used in the theme gallery also work only transiently in your
          browser's memory and are not stored permanently.
        </p>
        <p>
          Since we use no cookies and no comparable technologies, there is nothing here for you to
          object to. Should that change in future, for example by adding a more convenient feature
          that requires storage on your device, we would obtain consent under &sect; 25(1) TDDDG
          beforehand and amend this policy accordingly.
        </p>

        <h3>10.2 Security and abuse prevention</h3>
        <p>
          Our host applies technical protective measures to fend off attacks on the website and to
          keep it available. In doing so, the technical data described in section 4, in particular
          the IP address, may be processed and stored briefly in order to detect and block
          suspicious access patterns.
        </p>
        <p>
          The legal basis is Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR. Our legitimate interest lies in protecting the
          website, our systems and visitors from attack and abuse, and in keeping the site
          available. This processing is not based on consent.
        </p>

        <h3>10.3 Relationship to &sect; 25 TDDDG</h3>
        <p>
          The German Telecommunications Digital Services Data Protection Act requires consent under
          &sect; 25(1) TDDDG where, in the course of using a digital service, information is stored
          on the user's device or information already stored on the device is accessed, regardless
          of whether that information is personal data. &sect; 25(2) TDDDG exempts from that
          requirement storage and access that is strictly necessary to provide a digital service
          the user has expressly requested.
        </p>
        <p>
          For our processing this means in concrete terms: the website and its files are delivered
          without any access to your device. No cookies are set, no identifiers are stored, no
          storage is read and no script is run on your device for analytics purposes. The audience
          measurement described in section 6.2 evaluates only data that arises server-side at the
          host, and is therefore not an operation within the meaning of &sect; 25(1) TDDDG.
        </p>
        <p>
          Where processing requires no consent under &sect; 25 TDDDG, its lawfulness and its limits
          are then governed by the GDPR alone, here by Art. 6(1)(f) GDPR. For the avoidance of
          doubt, we would obtain consent as soon as we introduced technologies that store on or
          access your device and are not strictly necessary.
        </p>

        <h2>11. Third-country transfers and transfer mechanisms</h2>
        <p>
          Some of the service providers we use process personal data in the USA. A transfer to a
          third country is permitted under Art. 44 et seq. GDPR only where an adequate level of
          protection is ensured or one of the derogations applies. We take care that every transfer
          rests on a sound basis.
        </p>
        <p>
          Two mechanisms are used, in part cumulatively. First, the European Commission's adequacy
          decision on the EU-U.S. Data Privacy Framework. Where the recipient in question is
          certified under the Data Privacy Framework, the level of protection for that transfer is
          established. Second, the standard contractual clauses under Art. 46(2)(c) GDPR, which we
          conclude with our service providers and which establish an adequate level of protection
          as appropriate safeguards.
        </p>

