        <p>
          Before a transfer takes place we check that the conditions still hold. We follow
          developments concerning the transfer mechanisms, in particular the adequacy decisions on
          the Data Privacy Framework, and adapt our agreements if the legal position changes. If a
          transfer could no longer rest on a sound basis, we would change the service or stop the
          processing.
        </p>
        <p>
          A particularity applies to the recipients that are independent controllers: with GitHub
          the transfer happens because your browser requests the page from GitHub. We do not
          transmit the data to GitHub, we merely embed something that causes your browser to do so.
          We have no influence over GitHub's specific processing; we chose GitHub as an established
          provider with high security standards and clear data protection commitments.
        </p>
        <p>
          We cannot rule out that US authorities may access data processed by these providers on
          the basis of US law. That is why we base the transfer on an adequacy decision or on
          standard contractual clauses, and transmit no data that is not necessary for the purposes
          of the website.
        </p>

        <h2>12. Your rights under Art. 12 to 22 GDPR</h2>
        <p>
          Under Art. 12 GDPR we inform you about the processing of your data in a concise,
          transparent, intelligible and easily accessible form, using clear and plain language. The
          list below explains the individual rights you have under the GDPR and how to exercise
          them. Exercising these rights is free of charge for you.
        </p>

        <h3>12.1 Transparency and information (Art. 12, 13 and 14 GDPR)</h3>
        <p>
          You have the right to be informed about the processing of personal data in a compact
          form. This policy fulfils the obligations under Art. 13 GDPR for the data we process. If
          we were in future to collect data not obtained directly from you, we would inform you
          under Art. 14 GDPR within the statutory periods, and at the latest within one month,
          about the origin, categories and purposes of that processing.
        </p>

        <h3>12.2 Right of access (Art. 15 GDPR)</h3>
        <p>
          You have the right to obtain confirmation from us as to whether we process personal data
          concerning you. Where that is the case, you are entitled to information about
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>the purposes of the processing;</li>
          <li>the categories of personal data processed;</li>
          <li>
            the recipients or categories of recipient to whom the data has been or will be
            disclosed, including recipients in third countries;
          </li>
          <li>
            the envisaged storage period or, where that is not possible, the criteria used to
            determine it;
          </li>
          <li>
            the existence of a right to rectification, erasure, restriction of processing or
            objection;
          </li>
          <li>the existence of a right to lodge a complaint with a supervisory authority;</li>
          <li>the origin of the data, where it was not collected from you;</li>
          <li>
            the existence of automated decision-making including profiling, together with
            meaningful information about the logic involved.
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          You are also entitled under the second half-sentence of Art. 15(1) GDPR to a free copy of
          the personal data undergoing processing. We provide the first copy free of charge; for
          further copies we may charge a reasonable fee based on administrative costs. Where you
          make a request electronically, we provide the information in a commonly used electronic
          format where possible.
        </p>

        <h3>12.3 Right to rectification (Art. 16 GDPR)</h3>
        <p>
          You have the right to obtain without undue delay the rectification of inaccurate personal
          data concerning you. Taking the purposes of the processing into account, you also have
          the right to have incomplete personal data completed, including by means of a
          supplementary statement. Please tell us which detail is wrong or incomplete; we will
          review the request and rectify the data where it applies.
        </p>

        <h3>12.4 Right to erasure (Art. 17 GDPR)</h3>
        <p>
          You have the right to obtain from us the erasure of personal data concerning you without
          undue delay, and we are obliged to erase the data without undue delay, where one of the
          following grounds applies:
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>the data is no longer necessary for the purposes for which it was collected;</li>
          <li>
            you object to the processing and there are no overriding legitimate grounds on our
            side;
          </li>
          <li>the data was processed unlawfully;</li>
          <li>erasure is required to comply with a legal obligation.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          The right to erasure does not apply where processing is necessary for exercising the
          right of freedom of expression and information, for compliance with a legal obligation,
          for reasons of public interest, or for the establishment, exercise or defence of legal
          claims. In practice this concerns above all statutory retention obligations for business
          correspondence; in those cases we restrict the processing instead of erasing, and tell
          you so.
        </p>

        <h3>12.5 Right to restriction of processing (Art. 18 GDPR)</h3>
        <p>
          You have the right to obtain restriction of processing where one of the following
          applies:
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            you contest the accuracy of the data, for a period enabling us to verify its accuracy;
          </li>
          <li>
            the processing is unlawful and you oppose erasure and request restriction of use
            instead;
          </li>
          <li>
            we no longer need the data for the purposes of the processing, but you need it for the
            establishment, exercise or defence of legal claims;
          </li>
          <li>
            you have objected under Art. 21(1) GDPR and it has not yet been established whether our
            legitimate grounds override yours.
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          Where processing is restricted, we may, apart from storing the data, process it only with
          your consent, for the establishment, exercise or defence of legal claims, for the
          protection of the rights of another natural or legal person, or for reasons of important
          public interest. We will tell you before the restriction is lifted.
        </p>

        <h3>12.6 Notification obligation on rectification or erasure (Art. 19 GDPR)</h3>
        <p>
          We communicate any rectification, erasure or restriction of processing to each recipient
          to whom we have disclosed personal data, unless this proves impossible or involves
          disproportionate effort. We will inform you about those recipients if you ask.
        </p>

        <h3>12.7 Right to data portability (Art. 20 GDPR)</h3>
        <p>
          You have the right to receive the personal data concerning you that you have provided to
          us in a structured, commonly used and machine-readable format, and to transmit that data
          to another controller without hindrance, where the processing is based on consent or on a
          contract and is carried out by automated means. You also have the right to have the data
          transmitted directly to another controller where technically feasible.
        </p>
        <p>
          Please note that this right applies only to data you provided to us yourself and that we
          process by automated means. Among the processing described in this policy, that concerns
          above all the contents of a contact enquiry. Where processing is based on Art. 6(1)(f)
          GDPR, the right to portability under Art. 20(1)(a) GDPR does not apply; in that case the
          right of access under Art. 15 GDPR is what helps you.
        </p>

        <h3>12.8 Right to object (Art. 21 GDPR)</h3>
        <p>
          You have the right to object at any time, on grounds relating to your particular
          situation, to processing of personal data concerning you that is based on Art. 6(1)(f)
          GDPR. That covers all processing in this policy based on legitimate interest, in
          particular delivery of the website, hosting of the documentation and the gallery,
          audience measurement, fending off attacks, and displaying project information and
          downloads.
        </p>
        <p>
          We will then no longer process the personal data concerned, unless we can demonstrate
          compelling legitimate grounds for the processing which override your interests, rights
          and freedoms, or the processing serves the establishment, exercise or defence of legal
          claims. Where processing were for direct marketing purposes, you could object at any time
          without giving reasons; no such processing currently takes place.
        </p>

        <h3>12.9 Automated decisions and profiling (Art. 22 GDPR)</h3>
        <p>
          You have the right not to be subject to a decision based solely on automated processing,
          including profiling, which produces legal effects concerning you or similarly
          significantly affects you. We use no such decision-making. No profiling and no automated
          evaluation of you takes place. Were we in future to use procedures falling under Art. 22
          GDPR, we would inform you beforehand and make available the options in Art. 22(3) GDPR,
          namely human intervention, the expression of your point of view and the right to contest
          the decision.
        </p>

        <h3>12.10 Withdrawal of consent, and limitations</h3>
        <p>
          Where processing is based on consent, you can withdraw it at any time (Art. 7(3) GDPR).
          Withdrawal does not affect the lawfulness of processing carried out before it. Withdrawal
          must be as easy as giving consent. We currently base no processing on consent, so there
          is presently nothing to withdraw.
        </p>
        <p>
          The rights under the GDPR are subject to the limitations in Art. 12(5) GDPR (protection
          against manifestly unfounded or excessive requests) and to the statutory limitations
          under Art. 23 GDPR, &sect;&sect; 34 and 35 BDSG and other sector-specific provisions. We
          rely on limitations only where that is legally permissible and necessary, and we give
          reasons for refusals.
        </p>

        <h3>12.11 Time limits and response format</h3>
        <p>
          We answer requests without undue delay and in any event within one month of receipt
          (Art. 12(3) GDPR). That period may be extended by a further two months for complex
          requests or a large number of requests; in that case we inform you within one month of
          receipt about the extension and the reasons for it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Where there are reasonable doubts about your identity, we may request additional
          information necessary to confirm it under Art. 12(6) GDPR. We use that information solely
          for that purpose and delete it afterwards.
        </p>
        <p>
          If we do not act on your request, we inform you without undue delay, and at the latest
          within one month of receipt, of the reasons, and point out that you may lodge a complaint
          with a supervisory authority and that judicial remedies are available to you.
        </p>

        <h3>12.12 Right to lodge a complaint with a supervisory authority (Art. 77 GDPR)</h3>
        <p>
          Without prejudice to any other remedy, you have the right to lodge a complaint with a
          supervisory authority, in particular in the member state of your habitual residence,
          place of work or the place of the alleged infringement, if you consider that the
          processing of personal data concerning you infringes the GDPR. The supervisory authority
          responsible for us is:
        </p>
        <p>
          Landesbeauftragte f&uuml;r Datenschutz und Informationsfreiheit Nordrhein-Westfalen<br />
          Kavalleriestra&szlig;e 2 to 4<br />
          40213 D&uuml;sseldorf, Germany<br />
          <a href="https://ldi.nrw.de" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener">ldi.nrw.de</a>
        </p>
        <p>
          You may also approach any other supervisory authority in the European Union, in
          particular the authority where you live or work. The right to complain is available to
          you at any time and without contacting us first; we would nonetheless be glad if you came
          to us first, so we can resolve your concern quickly.
        </p>

        <h3>12.13 Exercising your rights</h3>
        <p>
          To exercise any of these rights, a message to
          <a href="mailto:privacy@pelton.app">privacy@pelton.app</a> is enough.
        </p>
        <p>
          Please make clear which right you wish to exercise and which operation your request
          relates to. Providing evidence that the details you give are correct will speed up
          handling. We treat requests confidentially and process the data needed to handle them
          solely for that purpose.
        </p>
        <p>
          Where your request concerns processing for which not we but one of our service providers
          is independently responsible, such as the logging of access data by GitHub, we forward
          your request to the responsible controller where that is permissible and possible, and
          tell you that we have done so.
        </p>

        <h2>13. Data security</h2>
        <p>
          Under Art. 32 GDPR we take technical and organisational measures to ensure a level of
          protection appropriate to the risk. These include in particular delivery of the website
          over an encrypted HTTPS/TLS connection, the use of established, security-reviewed
          infrastructure for hosting and email, restricting access to personal data to the people
          who need it, and selecting service providers according to their security commitments.
        </p>
        <p>
          Please note that transmitting data over the internet can never be made entirely secure.
          We protect the path between your browser and our host with transport encryption. You are
          responsible for the security of your own device, browser and email account.
        </p>

        <h2>14. Changes to this policy, and contact</h2>
        <p>
          We may amend this policy to reflect changes to the website, to the services we use or to
          the legal position. The current version is always available at pelton.app/privacy/de.
          Amendments apply going forward; earlier versions can be requested from us. We mark each
          version with a date so you can see when it was last changed.
        </p>
        <p>
          The terms of use and liability notices at <a href="/terms">pelton.app/terms</a> belong
          with this version. They sit alongside this policy and do not concern the processing of
          personal data.
        </p>

